Apply NICE= from crontabs as a nice(1) increment, after switching to the
job user; without it NICE=15 in PLD's /etc/cron.d/crontab does nothing.

Why the autogroup: every job calls setsid(), and with
CONFIG_SCHED_AUTOGROUP a new session is a new autogroup at nice 0.  In
the root CPU cgroup sessions compete as a whole, so the job's own nice
alone would not lower its CPU share against other sessions (sched(7)).

How: after nice() the job writes its niceness to /proc/self/autogroup.
The file is opened before dropping root, as /proc/self is root-owned
afterwards; the kernel checks the written value against the job user.
Writes without CAP_SYS_ADMIN are limited to one per 100 ms system-wide
(EAGAIN), which comes only after the permission checks, so on EAGAIN the
job passes the value to its crond parent, still root, to repeat it, and
waits until crond closes the socket, so it never starts in an autogroup
at nice 0.

diff -urNp cronie-1.7.2.orig/man/crontab.5 cronie-1.7.2/man/crontab.5
--- cronie-1.7.2.orig/man/crontab.5	2024-04-08 10:06:00.000000000 +0200
+++ cronie-1.7.2/man/crontab.5	2026-10-30 09:20:12.455885861 +0100
@@ -157,6 +157,27 @@ upper limit specified by the variable. T
 determined during the cron daemon startup so it remains constant for
 the whole run time of the daemon.
 .PP
+The
+.I NICE
+variable adjusts the niceness of jobs by the given increment, as
+.B nice \-n
+does (see
+.BR nice (1));
+the resulting niceness is limited to the range from \-20 to 19.
+The increment is applied after switching to the job user, so a negative
+value works only for root or within the user's
+.B RLIMIT_NICE
+(see
+.BR setrlimit (2)).
+The job's session gets the same autogroup nice value, so the job also
+yields to other sessions; in a cgroup with the cpu controller, niceness
+only ranks the job against processes of the same cgroup (see
+.BR sched (7)).
+An invalid value is logged when the crontab is loaded and ignored.
+If the kernel refuses the adjustment, the job keeps its niceness; if it
+refuses only the autogroup change, the job runs with the new niceness in
+an autogroup at nice 0.  Both are logged when the job starts.
+.PP
 The format of a cron command is similar to the V7 standard, with a number
 of upward-compatible extensions.  Each line has five time-and-date fields
 followed by a
